<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Performance evaluation on the <span id="Sx4.T5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LibriSpeech test-clean</span> across different noise scaling factor <math id="Sx4.T5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> using 5-second audio prompts. The NFE is set to 64 as default.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Temporal Diversity Analysis</span>.
Table <a href="#Sx4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Main Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Flamed-TTS: Flow Matching Attention-Free Models for Efficient Generating and Dynamic Pacing Zero-shot Text-to-Speech" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> analyzes temporal diversity across models. Compared to baselines with deterministic duration predictors (e.g., NaturalSpeech 2, OZSpeech), <span id="Sx4.SSx2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Flamed-TTS</span> exhibits greater diversity and naturalness. It generates slower speech rates and higher MPhD, both with higher variability. Notably, its number of pauses and MPaD are 4× and 5× higher, respectively, indicating more spontaneous, human-like speech.
F5-TTS performs similarly in temporal diversity, suggesting its architecture and training paradigm remain effective in this regard. However, it lags behind <span id="Sx4.SSx2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Flamed-TTS</span> in WER due to the lack of phoneme-speech alignment. These findings highlight that employing <span id="Sx4.SSx2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Probabilistic Duration and Silence Generators</span> can help close the dynamic pacing gap between AR and NAR TTS models, while also enhancing phoneme-to-speech alignment for improved intelligibility.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Ablation Study</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NFE Evaluation</span>.
We evaluate the performance of <span id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Flamed-TTS</span> under varying numbers of function evaluations (NFE). Results from Table <a href="#Sx4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Main Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Flamed-TTS: Flow Matching Attention-Free Models for Efficient Generating and Dynamic Pacing Zero-shot Text-to-Speech" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> show that SIM-O and SIM-R peak at NFE=16, while WER increases slightly (by  1%) at NFE=4 but remains stable around 4% as NFE increases. In contrast, UTMOS consistently improves with higher NFE, indicating enhanced naturalness with more denoising steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Noise Scaling Effect</span>.
In contrast to NFE, increasing the noise scaling factor <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> does not consistently improve performance. As shown in Table <a href="#Sx4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Main Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Flamed-TTS: Flow Matching Attention-Free Models for Efficient Generating and Dynamic Pacing Zero-shot Text-to-Speech" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, the model achieves optimal results across all metrics at <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.3</annotation></semantics></math>. From <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.3</annotation></semantics></math>, UTMOS, SIM-O, and SIM-R show modest improvements; however, further increases in <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> beyond 0.3 result in performance degradation.</p>
</div>
